-From Hand-Drawn Schematics to SPICE Netlists: A
-Deterministic Pipeline with Endpoint-Graph Wire
-Joining and a Human-Verified Connectivity
-Benchmark
-Bosco Chanam, Chris Dcosta, Pranavesh Kumar Talupuri, Shwetambari A. Chiwhane, Ashay Kumar Singh, and
-Arghadeep Das
-
-Abstract—Automated digitization of hand-drawn circuit
-schematics into SPICE-format netlists remains challenging because connectivity recovery is brittle under noisy wire detections.
-We present a deterministic pipeline that converts a scanned
-schematic into a structural SPICE-format netlist through component detection, an occlusion-first wire extractor, and a graphbased join that resolves electrical connectivity; component values
-are not read from the image and are supplied by the user
-or a downstream value-reader. The main contribution is the
-joining stage: an endpoint-graph model over wire endpoints and
-component pins with five edge types, combined with degreebudget completion that recovers dropped connections through
-min-cost b-matching while bounding over-merge with per-pin
-budgets and self-loop guards. We also introduce a synthetic
-perturbation protocol and, to our knowledge, the first humanverified net-level connectivity benchmark for hand-drawn circuits, covering 31 images from CGHD-1152. The occlusion-first
-extractor reaches wire-detection F1 = 0.976 on 134 scanned
-images. On our 31-image, CGHD-1152-sourced human-verified
-net-level benchmark, the best join reaches connectivity microF1 = 0.890, outperforming the prior completion default (0.83),
-a legacy radius union-find join (0.67), a connected-component
-net-tracing baseline (0.62), and a Hough-plus-proximity line
-tracer (0.81), all under identical detected wires. Running the
-join on perfect wire labels does not improve micro-F1, showing
-that connectivity, not wire detection, remains the bottleneck.
-A first-party comparison shows a strong vision-language model
-reaches 0.923 on the same images, statistically indistinguishable
-from our join but far more expensive and without structural
-guarantees, supporting explicit geometric connectivity recovery
-for deployable circuit digitization.
-Index Terms—circuit digitization, connectivity benchmark,
-graph-based connectivity, hand-drawn circuits, image-to-netlist,
-netlist extraction, schematic recognition, SPICE, wire extraction,
-wire joining
