-V. D ISCUSSION
-The endpoint-graph model resolves ambiguity that radiusbased methods cannot: by representing both wire endpoints
-and component pins as nodes with typed edges, it distinguishes
-between a wire that passes near a component (endpoint–wirebody edge) and one that connects to it (endpoint–pin edge
-with directional preference). The component-first assignment
-further disambiguates by using the geometric constraint that
-wires must reach a component’s pin, not just its bounding box.
-Degree-budget completion addresses the fundamental limitation of detector noise: even a 97.6% accurate wire detector
-drops ∼2% of wire endpoints, and those dropped endpoints
-are precisely the connections that matter most for simulation
-accuracy. The b-matching formulation bounds the damage
-from mis-detection (each floating pin gets at most one recovery
-edge), while the self-loop guard prevents the catastrophic
-short-circuit failures that plague unconstrained approaches.
-The human-verified net-level evaluation, while novel for
-hand-drawn circuits, currently covers 31 images; the 95%
-bootstrap confidence intervals on the per-strategy F1 are
-correspondingly wide (±0.03–0.05) and overlap between adjacent strategies, so while the top ranking is consistent and
-corroborated by the independently authored synthetic suite,
-broader annotation is needed to separate the closely ranked
-configurations. The net-level labels were bootstrapped by
-a conservative endpoint-graph join over perfect wires and
-then human-corrected; we mitigate the residual concern that
-this favors graph-based methods by validating on synthetic
-ground truth that is authored from scratch, where the same
-ranking holds. Annotation was performed by a single human
-annotator using a purpose-built verification tool, and no perimage timing log was retained, so a per-image annotation-cost
-figure cannot be reported. The evaluation draws from a single
-hand-drawn corpus (CGHD-1152); generalization across other
-drawing styles, capture conditions, and printed schematics remains to be validated. The pipeline emits SPICE-active netlist
-pins only for the device classes with connectivity-verified
-pin templates (resistor, capacitor, inductor, diode, transistor,
-voltage source, and IC-as-black-box); complex devices such
-as transformers, thyristors, optocouplers, triacs, relays, and
-switches are detected and located but their pin relationships
-are not extracted, so they are excluded from the net-level
-benchmark and from netlist emission. Component detection at
-88.5% mean average precision (mAP@0.5) also leaves room
-for improvement, particularly on crossovers (70.7% recall),
-which matter beyond detection because the join itself is classagnostic: edge types 2, 4, and 5 bridge wire fragments by
-pure geometry, with no reference to component class, so where
-two wires cross at a detected crossover symbol the endpoint
-graph can treat them as a single meeting point. We quantified
-this exposure with a causal ablation on the benchmark: every
-geometric edge that formed near a ground-truth crossover was
-identified and suppressed in isolation, and the image’s falsepositive component pairs were re-checked. Across the 8 of
-
-9
-
-31 images containing a crossover (13 instances total), exactly
-one confirmed false merge was found—on C242 D1 P1,
-where stub endpoints from three wire fragments land within
-the join tolerance at the crossing and a single edge type2 union is the sole cause of 4 false component pairs; two
-other crossover-adjacent edges were ruled out by the same
-test (one over-merge persists through an unrelated path, one
-merge is actually correct), and the remaining five crossovercontaining images had no bridging edge form near the crossing
-at all. The failure is therefore real but rare on this benchmark
-(1/31 images, 3.2%; 1/8 crossover-bearing images), and it is
-disclosed rather than hidden. A targeted mitigation—excluding
-wire stubs that fall inside a detected crossover bounding box
-from edge types 2/4/5—is identified but not yet implemented
-or benchmarked; no projected improvement is claimed. The
-VLM connectivity comparison uses a single frontier model
-(Claude Opus 4.8) on the same 31 images; evaluating additional models and prompting strategies is future work. The
-component-detection model was trained on CGHD-1152 after
-excluding drafter 0, whose drawing style diverged markedly
-from the other drafters; this exclusion reduces the training
-set but avoids domain-shift artifacts from an outlier style.
-Finally, the synthetic error model is a first-pass approximation
-of detector noise rather than a calibrated fit; with the new realimage net-level metric available, calibrating it to the measured
-detector failure distribution is a natural next step.
-VI. C ONCLUSION
-We present a complete deterministic pipeline for handdrawn circuit digitization, built on an occlusion-first wire
-extractor that yields an endpoint representation and a two-stage
-endpoint-graph join, together with, to our knowledge, the first
-human-verified net-level connectivity benchmark for handdrawn circuits. The endpoint-graph join with degree-budget
-completion is most robust under synthetic error injection
-(F1 = 0.95 at the highest severity vs. 0.36 for the radius baseline), and, paired with a high-precision scale-relative base, attains connectivity micro-F1 = 0.890 on our 31-image, CGHD1152-sourced benchmark against human-verified ground truth,
-beating the prior completion default (0.83) and every deterministic baseline, including the connected-component nettracing recipe used by recent systems (0.62) and a Hough-plusproximity line tracer (0.81), all on identical detected wires.
-Running the join on perfect wires does not raise micro-F1 at
-all, showing the join, not wire detection, is the bottleneck.
-A first-party experiment with a frontier VLM reaches 0.923
-on the same images, a difference from our join that is not
-statistically significant (paired 95% CI [−0.009, +0.078]), but
-at two to three orders of magnitude higher cost and without structurally valid, machine-checkable output, supporting
-the explicit geometric design. Future work includes scaling
-the human-verified benchmark, calibrating the synthetic error
-model to the measured detector distribution, and extending
-coverage to devices outside the simulatable set.
